# 2026-09-08 (session 79): A DEFER-UNTIL, and the FIRST time this mechanism has
#   been used since 2026-08-19. Read the reason before extending the date.
#
#   WHAT CHANGED: the lone-CR line-accounting fix, which IS a real extraction
#   change. A CR that is not part of a CRLF terminates a line for Delphi, the
#   RAD Studio IDE, VS Code and .NET, but tree-sitter advances its row counter
#   on LF only -- so every line the indexer stored after such a byte was one
#   lower than the editor shows. Two files in the hashed surface:
#     src\preprocess\DRagLint.Preprocess.Types.pas  -- NormalizeLoneCR (new)
#     src\parser\DRagLint.Parser.Delphi13.pas       -- calls it before parsing
#   Stored POSITIONS therefore change for any indexed file holding a lone CR, so
#   a bump is genuinely owed. This is not an escape-hatch "cannot affect
#   extraction" entry; it is a postponement of a debt that is real.
#
#   WHY IT IS DEFERRED RATHER THAN BUMPED NOW. The bump is batched, by the
#   owner's standing instruction: INBOX-extractor-change-bank.md holds TWO rows
#   and only this one is built. The other -- M1b, a body-inline comment corpus
#   written at index time -- is unbuilt. Bumping today would bill the full ~5 h
#   re-parse of ~7,000 library files plus every project index and bank only half
#   the batch, which is exactly what PLAN-SESSION-79 forbids: "If they are not
#   both finished and guarded by then, do not bump and do not start the
#   reindex."
#
#   WHAT IS STALE MEANWHILE, stated plainly because a deferral is a live debt:
#   every existing index keeps the OLD (one-too-low) line numbers for the files
#   that contain a lone CR. Measured across all 34 indexes on this machine:
#   58 files of 11,370, of which only 7 are code and 6 of those are vendored --
#   ABC5, kbmMemTable, OmniThreadLibrary and a CAD library. Exactly one is code
#   this shop owns (DataCopy Tests\Test.UsageWiring.pas, one lone CR).
#
#   AND THE USER-VISIBLE HALF IS ALREADY FIXED WITHOUT THIS BUMP, which is why
#   deferring costs so little. There are THREE parse entry points and only one
#   is in this surface:
#     TDelphi13Parser.Parse       (src\parser)      -- the indexer   <- deferred
#     TLinter                     (src\lint)        -- own TTSParser <- SHIPPED
#     TAstParseCache.GetOrParse   (src\diagnostics) -- AST checks    <- SHIPPED
#   Every harm the defect note lists -- `allow --fix-line` writing a review
#   marker to the wrong line, gutter icons, the Problems panel,
#   review-marker-stale hashing the wrong line -- comes from the latter two,
#   which are OUTSIDE this surface and shipped today with no reindex. Both get
#   the transform in TAstParseCache.ApplyPreprocess.
#
#   THE TRANSITIONAL INCONSISTENCY, so it is not discovered as a surprise: until
#   the bump, `lint` reports the editor-correct line for a lone-CR file while the
#   INDEX still holds the old one. Two surfaces disagreeing by one line, on the
#   seven files above. That is the price of the deferral and it resolves on the
#   reindex.
#
#   THE DATE. PLAN-SESSION-79 has the owner kicking off the ~5 h reindex on
#   Tuesday evening 2026-09-09, with the bump immediately before it. 2026-09-10
#   gives that one day of slack so an overnight run cannot turn the battery red
#   mid-flight. The guard goes RED by itself on 2026-09-11, which is the point.
#
#   Pinned by tests\autotest\run_lone_cr_line_accounting.ps1, which was
#   observed RED (13,17) against the pre-fix engine and green (14,18) after.

# 2026-09-08 (session 79): A REAL BUMP, 1.10.0-alpha -> 1.11.0-alpha, and it
#   DISCHARGES the DEFER-UNTIL|2026-09-10 recorded earlier the same day. Both
#   rows of INBOX-extractor-change-bank.md are now built and guarded, which was
#   the condition that deferral was waiting on -- so the bump is taken once, for
#   both, exactly as the bank exists to arrange.
#
#   WHAT CHANGED, and each genuinely invalidates stored parses:
#
#   1. LONE-CR LINE ACCOUNTING (src\preprocess\DRagLint.Preprocess.Types.pas,
#      new NormalizeLoneCR; called from src\parser\DRagLint.Parser.Delphi13.pas).
#      tree-sitter advances its row counter on LF only, so a CR that is not part
#      of a CRLF did not start a new line for it while Delphi, the IDE, VS Code
#      and .NET all treat it as a terminator. Every POSITION stored after such a
#      byte was one line low. 58 of 11,370 indexed files hold a lone CR.
#
#   2. A COMMENT CORPUS (same parser unit, HarvestStringLiterals). Comment nodes
#      are now harvested into string_literals as kind 'comment' (// and the block
#      forms) and 'doc' (triple-slash), so `query --text` finds prose. Those are
#      NEW ROWS that no existing index contains -- the clearest possible case of
#      "an extractor emits new symbols/refs", and unreachable without a re-parse.
#
#   NO SCHEMA_VERSION BUMP IS OWED. The comments are rows in the EXISTING
#   string_literals table, so the existing string_literals_ai trigger mirrors
#   them into both string_fts (unicode61) and string_fts_tri (trigram). No new
#   table, no new trigger, no new column.
#
#   SIZE, MEASURED RATHER THAN FEARED. On src\parser (5 files, this repo's own
#   heavily-commented source) comment text is 96.6% of all indexed text and
#   raises it 29x -- which sounds alarming and is the wrong denominator. The
#   DATABASE grows **1.5%** (2,179,072 -> 2,211,840 bytes, both VACUUMed), because
#   a DB is dominated by symbols, refs and their indexes, not by literal text.
#   Measured by deleting the comment rows from a copy and vacuuming, which fires
#   the same triggers and reproduces a comments-free index exactly.
#
#   THE RE-PARSE IS THE POINT, not a cost to argue away: until every index is
#   rebuilt, a lone-CR file answers with line numbers one low, and NO index can
#   answer a comment-prose query at all.
#
#   Pinned by tests\autotest\run_lone_cr_line_accounting.ps1 (RED 13,17 before /
#   green 14,18 after, with an LF twin as oracle) and
#   tests\autotest\run_comment_corpus.ps1 (RED hits=0 before / 16 checks green
#   after, with literal-vs-comment --kind controls both ways).
#
#   Superseded: 1.10.0-alpha|7562d480be39c02cdf165f9bf4edf888a158742de7a2b68468a0e1e2eff637d2

# 2026-09-08: bumped 1.11.0-alpha -> 1.12.0-alpha. TDocCommentScanner counted
#   lines on #10 ONLY, so a LONE CR left it one line behind TStringList and the
#   store (CR-aware since session 79). Doc-block replacement ranges are computed
#   from those numbers, so `document --apply` DELETED the declaration packed above
#   a doc block. Reported from DataCopy; reproduced in 30 lines. This changes which
#   line every doc comment is reported on, so it is a genuine extraction change.
# 2026-09-08 (second bump, same session): 1.12.0-alpha -> 1.13.0-alpha. The DFM
#   parser now emits the `object` line's TYPE as a searchable `dfm-type` text row,
#   so `query --text "TOvcTable" --source dfm` can answer which forms hold a
#   component of a type (it returned 0 against 28 real blocks). TWO bumps in one
#   day is deliberate and costs nothing: 1.12.0-alpha was never released and no
#   index was ever built against it, so both changes ride ONE re-parse. Bumping
#   again was preferred over editing the hash under the old version, which would
#   have meant asserting "cannot affect extraction" about a change that plainly
#   does.
# 2026-09-09: hash updated WITHOUT a version bump, per the rule above.
#   src\index\DRagLint.Index.Reconcile.pas changed: TProjectReconciler.Apply now
#   REFUSES non-.pas closure entries (the {$I} includes) instead of splicing them
#   into a uses clause as `QDefs in 'QDefs.inc'`, which does not compile; plus a
#   new TReconcileApplyResult.Refused field and comments.
#   NOT EXTRACTION, and that was checked rather than asserted: TProjectReconciler
#   is instantiated ONLY by DRagLint.CLI.DoReconcileProject, never on the
#   indexing path, and the change touches only the record and the Apply body. It
#   cannot alter which symbols/refs/uses/call edges any index receives.
#   Reconcile lives under src\index and so falls inside this guard's surface,
#   but it is a project-MEMBERSHIP verb, not an extractor -- the same reasoning
#   as the 2026-08-24 ManifestWrite entry above.
#   A bump here would force a full re-parse of all 35 indexes; the previous one
#   had just finished at 01:21 the same night, costing 5h32m.
# 2026-09-09 (second entry, same day): bumped 1.13.0-alpha -> 1.14.0-alpha.
#   WHAT CHANGED: exactly one type, in TryWalkEnum
#   (src\parser\DRagLint.Parser.Delphi13.pas) -- the running enum-ordinal
#   counter went Integer -> Int64, plus its explanatory comment.
#   WHY IT IS EXTRACTION, and it is not a close call: the counter holds
#   'last ordinal + 1', which for the `= $7FFFFFFF` FORCE_DWORD idiom is
#   High(Integer)+1 and does not fit an Integer. The following Inc raised
#   EIntOverflow under the Debug build's DCC_IntegerOverflowCheck, and it threw
#   out of Parse BEFORE Core.Indexer OpenFileTx -- so the FILE never got a row.
#   The parser now produces a full parse for NINE units where it produced
#   NOTHING: Winapi.D3D10, Winapi.D3DCommon, Winapi.D3DCompiler, Winapi.D3DX10,
#   Winapi.DXGI1_2, WinAPI.Media, WinAPI.UI.Core and dxFontFile (x2 platforms).
#   That is the largest possible change in what extraction produces.
#   WHAT IS STALE UNTIL THE RE-PARSE: those nine units answer 0 rows in every
#   index -- and have since 18bbbc8 (2026-07-26), silently, with only a
#   `SKIP <path>: EIntOverflow` line and exit code 0 to show for it. Nothing
#   else is stale: Int64 widening changes no emitted signature below the
#   overflow point, so every file that indexes today is byte-identical after.
#   THE CHEAPER PATH WAS CONSIDERED AND REJECTED, so this is a decision and not
#   an oversight. Because every affected file has NO row it is never up-to-date
#   and is re-attempted every run, so a hash-only update plus one incremental
#   pass under the fixed engine would have closed the gap in minutes with no
#   stale parse left behind (the 2026-08-26 'membership, not content' shape).
#   Not taken: the owner's standing instruction for this defect is to ride a
#   bump, and the rule as written keys on 'the parser changes what it produces',
#   which this plainly does. A broad reading of the escape hatch is the drift
#   every entry above warns about.
#   Pinned by tests\autotest\run_enum_ordinal_overflow.ps1, MEASURED RED then
#   GREEN with the same runner: 8 PASS / 8 FAIL against a pre-fix engine copy
#   taken before any edit, 16 PASS / 0 FAIL against the fixed build. Its four
#   controls (ctA=0, ctB=128, ctC=129, and a symbol that does not exist) pass on
#   BOTH engines, so the two runs differ in the engine and nothing else.
#   Superseded: 1.13.0-alpha|c4b87ef16f585311d507c27d817a570d632f17c63b7b05b9eece170a41f76c67

# 2026-09-10 (session 87): A DEFER-UNTIL. The bump IS genuinely owed -- read the
#   reason before extending the date, and do not quietly delete the line.
#
#   WHAT CHANGED: ref-gap G2 in src\parser\DRagLint.Parser.Delphi13.pas. A
#   UNIT-QUALIFIED reference to a routine -- `Alpha.Beta.Tools.Proc` -- was not
#   recorded in `refs` AT ALL. Ref-gap G emits a member-access for the rhs of a
#   dotted access only when the lhs is a PLAIN IDENTIFIER, and its own comment
#   argued the deeper levels were covered "via the recursion below". They were
#   not: the recursion emits a ref for each level's OWN rhs, so `A.B.C.Proc`
#   yielded `read A` and `member-access B` and lost `C` and `Proc` entirely.
#   The parser now emits NEW REF ROWS that no existing index contains, which is
#   the clearest possible case of "an extractor emits different refs".
#
#   WHY IT MATTERS MORE THAN ITS SIZE SUGGESTS: `find-callers` UNDER-REPORTS on
#   that shape, and an under-report is worse than an error -- a zero reads as
#   "nothing calls this", which is the answer this index exists to be trusted
#   for. `unused-public-symbol` then fires on live code, the finding most likely
#   to get working code deleted. MEASURED on the plugin index, two assignments
#   on adjacent lines of DragLint.Plugin.Editor.pas:
#       GAfterSaveFanOutHook:= NotifyFanOutSave;                       -> 1 caller
#       AddWrappedItem(..., DragLint.Plugin.FanOut.InvokeCompileDependents); -> 0
#   Same routine kind, same file, same run; only the qualifier differs.
#
#   WHY DEFERRED RATHER THAN BUMPED NOW. The fix is wanted today; the ~5 h
#   re-parse of every index is not, and there is no second banked extractor
#   change to batch it with (INBOX-extractor-change-bank.md is gone -- session
#   79 discharged both of its rows). Bumping today would bill the full re-parse
#   for one change, on a day the owner is running in-IDE verification.
#
#   WHAT IS STALE MEANWHILE, stated plainly because a deferral is a live debt:
#   every existing index still MISSES every unit-qualified routine reference, so
#   `find-callers` and `unused-public-symbol` keep under-reporting on exactly
#   the shape this fixes. That is unchanged from before the fix -- the deferral
#   does not make anything worse, it just does not yet make it better.
#
#   THE CHEAP PARTIAL IS AVAILABLE AND DOES NOT NEED THE BUMP: a single
#   `index --force-reparse` (or a plain rebuild) of ONE project DB picks the new
#   refs up for that project in minutes. Do that for the plugin DB to close the
#   filed case; the global re-parse is what is being postponed.
#
#   THE DATE IS A PLACEHOLDER, and deliberately a near one: 2026-09-17 so the
#   debt cannot be forgotten. The owner should move it to whenever the next
#   library rebuild is actually planned, or discharge it with a real bump.
#
#   Pinned by tests\autotest\run_unit_qualified_routine_refs.ps1, MEASURED RED
#   then GREEN with the SAME runner and fixture and only the engine differing:
#   4 checks failed (1,2,3,6) against the pre-fix build with both controls
#   (4 = not a call kind, 5 = an unreferenced routine stays at zero) passing on
#   BOTH engines, and 7/7 green after.
# 2026-09-11 (session 88/89): the DEFER-UNTIL|2026-09-17 recorded above is
#   DISCHARGED. The owner authorised the bump and the full re-parse before
#   leaving, so ref-gap G2's unit-qualified refs are no longer carried as a
